<BaseLayout
  title="401(k) Employer Match & True-Up Explained (2027)"
  description="How front-loading your 401(k) can forfeit employer matching contributions if your plan does not offer a year-end true-up."
>
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>Employer Match &amp; True-Up Explained</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">
      <strong>Short answer:</strong> If your employer matches per paycheck and your plan does <em>not</em> have a year-end true-up, maxing out your 401(k) early can cause you to lose employer matching dollars on the remaining paychecks of 2027.
    </p>

    <div class="cta-back">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/" style="text-decoration: none;">Check Your Match &amp; True-Up in the Calculator →</a>
    </div>

    <h2>What a True-Up Does</h2>
    <p>
      Most 401(k) plans calculate employer match paycheck-by-paycheck (for example, 100% of your deferral up to 4% of that paycheck’s gross pay). If you hit the $25,500 IRS cap in October, your employee deferrals drop to $0 for November and December—so per-paycheck matching drops to $0 too.
    </p>
    <p>
      A <strong>year-end true-up</strong> reconciles your total annual compensation and total annual deferrals after December 31 and deposits the missing match. If your plan has no true-up (or you are not sure), spreading contributions evenly across every remaining paycheck protects your full match.
    </p>
  </article>

  <RelatedGuides excludeHref="/401k-match-true-up/" />
</BaseLayout>
